=== ARM 1: two placements, as 168 ran it ===
condition                   win chains  win inputs   chains where placement
                                                         changes the output
nearest (round to 2^3)               0           0                     2737
nearest (clamp)                      0           0                     1183
NOT nearest (truncate)              91        1330                     2296

=== ARM 2: every placement, as the claim states it ===
condition                   win chains  win inputs   chains where placement
                                                         changes the output
nearest (round to 2^3)               0           0                     2762
nearest (clamp)                      0           0                     1198
NOT nearest (truncate)             317       13527                     2297

C1 arm 1 reproduces 168's 0 / 0 / 91        : True   (got 0 / 0 / 91)
C2 truncate wins in both arms               : True   (91 narrow, 317 full)
C3 widening finds strictly more             : True   (317 > 91)

THE SIXTH INSTRUMENT DEFECT
  168's search compares two placements. Its claim quantifies over all of
  them. Widening the search to every placement takes the truncate control
  from 91 win-chains to 317, so intermediate placements do reach cases the
  endpoints do not: the instrument was thinner than the claim.

  AND THE CLAIM SURVIVES THE WIDER TEST.
  round: 0 win-chains over every placement. clamp: 0. Both still zero.
  The finding is not refuted; it is now supported at the strength it
  actually asserts rather than one placement's worth of it.


VERDICT
  round    : placement changes the output on 2762 of 3000 chains, and never helps.
  clamp    : placement changes the output on 1198 of 3000 chains, and never helps.
  truncate : placement changes the output on 2297 of 3000 chains, and helps on 317.

  The clamp row is exercised on 1198 chains against round's 2762. Both report
  zero, and they are not equally strong zeros: the smaller the set of
  chains where placement changes anything, the less a zero says.

  The matched pair is round against truncate: same representable set (the
  32 multiples of 8), differing only in nearest against not. That pair is
  a proper experiment and it carries the claim.
  The clamp row has a representable set of all 256 in-range values, so it
  is not the control's partner; it is a second positive with no matched
  negative. 168 does not claim otherwise, and its summary sentence
  ('checked over 3000 chains with a control that finds 1330
  counterexamples') reads as though the control covers both positives.
RC=0
